      "text": "Table T01 shows that L1 regression achieves 90.7% success on LIBERO-Long while generating 109.7 actions/s, compared with 91.1% and 4.2 actions/s for 50-step diffusion. Reducing diffusion to 10 steps raises throughput to 19.3 actions/s and lowers chunk latency from 1.9070 to 0.4145 s, with success at 91.0%. At one step, diffusion approaches L1's generation throughput (109.4 versus 109.7 actions/s) and chunk latency (0.0731 versus 0.0729 s), but success falls to 0.0%. Thus, the fastest diffusion setting does not preserve task success, whereas L1 combines high generation throughput with a similar reported success rate to multi-step diffusion. These measurements concern action generation, not end-to-end robot control; per-cell uncertainty and independent-run counts are not supplied."
    },
    {
      "task_id": "T02",
      "text": "Table T02. OpenVLA with parallel decoding (PD) and PD plus action chunking (PD+AC). Both generate 7-dimensional actions; AC generates eight actions per query. Throughput (actions/s; higher is better) and latency (s/query; lower is better) are averaged over 100 queries on an NVIDIA A100, each using one 224×224 image and a LIBERO task command. Latency covers one action for PD and an eight-action chunk for PD+AC. LIBERO-Long success (%; higher is better) uses 500 episodes (10 tasks × 50), with the best tested checkpoint selected per run. Independent training-run counts and per-cell uncertainty are not supplied. The success-rate dash denotes an unreported value, not zero or inapplicability. Throughput measures generation, not closed-loop control."
    },
    {
      "task_id": "T03",
      "text": "Table T03 shows different rankings for final average performance (AP) and online-learning regret in the six-task sequence. Perfect Memory attains the highest reported final AP (73.09%), compared with 72.93% for OA and 61.83% for Coreset. OA has the lowest regret (27.62%); Coreset and Perfect Memory reach 30.83% and 30.95%, respectively. AP averages success over all seen tasks at the final training step, whereas regret is the time-normalized accumulated current-task success shortfall relative to an oracle with success rate 1. Regret therefore is not the complement of final AP. These are joint agent/CEM-MPC outcomes, not isolated world-model prediction errors or memory/runtime measurements; per-cell uncertainty and significance tests are not supplied."
    },
    {
      "task_id": "T04",
      "text": "Table T04 shows higher exact-sequence success for GeoWorld than for V-JEPA 2 at each reported planning horizon under the shared ViT-g384 encoder scale. The difference is 1.55 percentage points at T=3 (51.71% versus 50.16%), 2.03 at T=4, 1.66 at T=5, and 1.38 at T=6. Success decreases with horizon for both methods, reaching 18.26% and 16.88%, respectively, at T=6. Both use frozen VideoMix22M-pretrained encoders and CEM planning. This comparison concerns predicted action sequences matching the ground truth given observation and goal clips; it does not establish physical execution performance, performance beyond T=6, a causal mechanism, or equal total training compute. Per-cell uncertainty is not supplied."
